  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "Which of the following are mathematical predicates of one variable?\n\n- $P(n):$ $n$ is even.\n- $Q(x):$ ${x}^{2}-x\\ge 1$\n- $R(y):$ Draw a line of width $y\\,\\text{cm}.$",
  "worked_solution": "A *predicate* of one variable is a sentence that contains the variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute any concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $n$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $x$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $y$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false. Rather, it will give an instruction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
